## What I checked, and how
- **R1 Data.** `report/code/fetch_data.py` reads the OpenML API (`/api/v1/json/data/61`) and checks OpenML's md5. My own download of
  https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff has md5 ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9, the same as the delivered `iris.arff`.
  The code asserts 150×4 and 50/50/50 classes, and nothing else loads Iris.
- **R2 Methods.** LR's `predict_proba` equals the softmax of its coefficients (so the model is multinomial), with C=1 fixed. kNN uses GridSearchCV over k=1..30 with an
  inner StratifiedKFold(5) on each outer training fold. Scaling sits inside both pipelines. The paper states all of this (§2).
- **R3 Protocol.** RepeatedStratifiedKFold(5, 10, seed 20261017) gives 50 distinct test sets of 30 with 120 training points, shared by both methods.
- **R4 Statistics.** Recomputed from `results/local/folds.csv`: LR 95.40 [91.56, 99.24], kNN 94.47 [89.46, 99.47],
  LR−kNN +0.93 [−2.51, +4.38] pp, all Nadeau–Bengio corrected t with df 49 and factor 1/50+30/120. These equal `intervals.csv`, `numbers.tex`, the table
  and the text. The naive t and fold-bootstrap intervals are labelled as contrast that ignores fold dependence. The conclusion
  ("not distinguishable") follows from the primary interval. Sensitivity claim checked in `results/sensitivity.json`: the corrected
  interval for the difference contains 0 for seeds 1–5, without scaling and with C tuned.
